# Two-Panel Hero: Slide-Ratio Containment (August 2026)

## What this rule covers

The homepage hero rendered by
[`src/components/modernist/ModernistPosterHero.tsx`](mdc:src/components/modernist/ModernistPosterHero.tsx)
is **not** a full-bleed background hero. It is a two-panel band:

```
┌───────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ logo card     │ rotating event poster (bleeds off the right │
│ (static,      │ edge of the viewport)                       │
│  contained)   │                                             │
└───────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                                    ↳ "View event →" below
```

Because the poster lives **inside a bounded panel** rather than behind the page, the
usual full-bleed hero advice does not apply. Read this rule before touching
`.mh-poster-hero-*` styles.

Styles live in [`src/styles/modernist-homepage.css`](mdc:src/styles/modernist-homepage.css)
under `body.modernist-home` (base) and `body.modernist-home.organic-home` (active theme).

## Before you start: confirm which hero you are editing

This repo contains a second, **unrendered** hero at `src/components/HeroSection.tsx`
using `.hero-slideshow-*` / `.hero-slide-image` classes in `globals.css`. Nothing
imports it. Edits there have no effect on the homepage and have already cost one
wasted debugging cycle.

- The homepage is `src/app/page.tsx` → `HomePageClient` → `ModernistHomePage` → `<ModernistPosterHero />`.
- **Homepage hero classes are `mh-poster-hero-*`.** If you are editing `.hero-slideshow-*`, you are in the wrong file.
- The hero is client-rendered, so it does **not** appear in `curl` output of `/`. Verify in the browser, or grep `.next/dev/static/chunks` to confirm CSS compiled.

## The core problem

Three facts collide:

1. **The panel width is viewport-driven.** The organic theme sets
   `.mh-poster-hero-inner { max-width: none; padding-right: 0 }` so the slide panel
   spans roughly 74% of the viewport and bleeds off the right edge. On a 6K monitor
   that is ~1800px wide.
2. **Slides are admin-uploaded** event posters. Observed ratios run from 16:9 up to
   ~3.2:1. There is no single ratio you can hard-code.
3. **A fixed panel height** (the old `min-height: min(64vh, 580px)`) combined with (1)
   produced a panel around 2:1 to 3.2:1 depending on the monitor.

Consequences of the naive fixes:

| Approach | Result |
|---|---|
| `object-fit: cover` | Poster scales to the panel width, then ~200px is sliced off top and bottom. Different crop on every monitor. This is the bug users report as "cut off on all 4 sides". |
| `object-fit: contain` alone | Nothing is cropped, but the poster shrinks to the fixed height and leaves large bare bars left and right. |
| Hard-coded `aspect-ratio: 16 / 9` | Correct for 16:9 slides only; every other slide letterboxes. |

## The solution: measure the slide, size the panel

**Panel ratio must equal poster ratio.** When they match, `object-fit: contain`
touches all four edges — no crop, no bars. Since the ratio varies per slide, measure
it at runtime.

### 1. Component reads the natural ratio on load

```tsx
const [slideRatio, setSlideRatio] = useState<number | null>(null);

<div
  className="mh-poster-hero-slide-panel"
  style={
    slideRatio
      ? ({ '--mh-hero-slide-ratio': String(slideRatio) } as React.CSSProperties)
      : undefined
  }
>
  <Image
    /* … fill, priority, sizes … */
    onLoad={(event) => {
      const { naturalWidth, naturalHeight } = event.currentTarget;
      if (naturalWidth > 0 && naturalHeight > 0) {
        setSlideRatio(naturalWidth / naturalHeight);
      }
    }}
  />
</div>
```

- **Update on `onLoad`, not on slide change.** Resetting to `null` when the index
  changes makes the panel snap to the fallback ratio and then to the real one — two
  visible resizes. Holding the previous ratio until the next image decodes means the
  panel resizes exactly once, in step with the crossfade.
- The value is a **unitless number**, which `aspect-ratio` accepts directly.

### 2. CSS takes the panel height from that ratio

```css
body.modernist-home.organic-home .mh-poster-hero-slide-panel {
  height: auto;
  align-self: start;
  aspect-ratio: var(--mh-hero-slide-ratio, 1.7778);
  max-height: min(70vh, 640px);
  overflow: hidden;
}

body.modernist-home.organic-home .mh-poster-hero-panels {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(200px, 26%) minmax(0, 1fr);
  align-items: stretch;
  min-height: 0; /* row height comes from the slide panel, not a viewport fraction */
}
```

Four details that are all load-bearing:

- **`height: auto` + no `height: 100%`.** A stretched grid item ignores its own
  `aspect-ratio`.
- **`align-self: start`.** Without it the default `stretch` re-expands the panel to the
  row height and the ratio is lost again.
- **`min-height: 0` on the panels grid.** Any `min-height` larger than the computed
  panel height leaves a dead gap below the panel (it is `align-self: start`).
- **`1.7778` fallback**, not `16 / 9`. Keep the fallback in the same unitless form as
  the runtime value so the two are interchangeable.

The logo panel keeps `height: 100%` and stretches to whatever row height the slide
panel produces, so the two panels stay flush.

### 3. Blurred backdrop covers the residual cases

`max-height` exists so the hero cannot swallow the viewport on an ultra-wide monitor.
When it clamps, the panel becomes wider than the poster and bars would reappear. A
second copy of the same image sits behind the poster, over-scaled and blurred, so the
panel always reads as full:

```tsx
<Image
  key={`backdrop-${current.url}-${index}`}
  src={current.url}
  alt=""
  aria-hidden
  fill
  loading="eager"
  sizes="480px"
  className={`mh-poster-hero-img mh-poster-hero-img-backdrop${fadeIn ? ' is-visible' : ''}`}
/>
```

```css
body.modernist-home .mh-poster-hero-media img.mh-poster-hero-img-backdrop {
  object-fit: cover;
  /* Scale must outrun the blur radius or the blur reveals the panel edges */
  transform: scale(1.25);
  filter: blur(30px) saturate(1.12);
  z-index: 0;
}

body.modernist-home .mh-poster-hero-media img.mh-poster-hero-img-backdrop.is-visible {
  opacity: 0.92;
}

body.modernist-home .mh-poster-hero-media img.mh-poster-hero-img:not(.mh-poster-hero-img-backdrop) {
  z-index: 1;
}
```

- **`scale` must exceed the blur radius as a fraction of panel width.** At `scale(1.25)`
  the image overhangs by 12.5% per side; on a 380px mobile panel that is ~47px against
  a 30px blur. Drop the scale and the blur will show transparent panel edges.
- **`sizes="480px"`** — the backdrop is blurred beyond recognition, so requesting a
  small source is free quality-wise and saves real bandwidth on a hero image.
- **`loading="eager"`**, not `priority`. Eager avoids a first-paint flash of bare panel
  behind the contained poster without adding a second `<link rel=preload>`.
- **Both layers carry `is-visible`** so the backdrop crossfades with the poster.

### 4. Specificity notes (these have bitten before)

Two existing rules fight the backdrop and must be out-specified, not reordered:

| Competing rule | Specificity | Backdrop selector | Specificity |
|---|---|---|---|
| `body.modernist-home.organic-home img { filter: none }` | (0,2,1) | `body.modernist-home .mh-poster-hero-media img.mh-poster-hero-img-backdrop` | (0,3,2) |
| `body.modernist-home .mh-poster-hero-media img { object-fit: contain !important }` (mobile block) | (0,2,2) | same selector with `!important` | (0,3,2) |

Always qualify the backdrop with **both** the `.mh-poster-hero-media` ancestor and the
`img` type, or `filter: none` will silently kill the blur.

## Mobile

The mobile block (`@media (max-width: 767px)`) stacks the panels and bleeds the slide
panel to both edges. It uses the same custom property:

```css
body.modernist-home.organic-home .mh-poster-hero-slide-panel {
  height: auto;
  aspect-ratio: var(--mh-hero-slide-ratio, 1.7778);
  max-height: 62vh;
  width: calc(100% + 16px);
  margin-left: -16px;
  border-radius: 0;
}

body.modernist-home .mh-poster-hero-media img,
body.modernist-home .mh-poster-hero-img {
  object-fit: contain !important;
  object-position: center center !important;
}

body.modernist-home .mh-poster-hero-media img.mh-poster-hero-img-backdrop {
  object-fit: cover !important;
}
```

The tighter `62vh` cap matters more on phones: a 3.2:1 banner is naturally short, but a
near-square poster on a 390px-wide screen would otherwise run past the fold.

## Accepted trade-offs

State these when someone asks why the hero looks taller than the mock:

1. **Filling the width without cropping requires height.** `height = width / ratio` is
   arithmetic, not a style preference. A wider panel is a taller panel.
2. **The hero height changes between slides** by design, since each poster gets a frame
   cut to its own shape. The crossfade absorbs this; do not "fix" it by re-pinning the
   height.
3. **The "Buy Tickets" overlay is pinned to the panel corner**, so when `max-height`
   clamps it floats over the blurred area rather than over the poster. It keeps its
   drop-shadow and stays legible. Making it track the poster's rendered box would
   require measuring the contained image rect — only worth it if someone complains.

## Checklist before shipping a change here

- [ ] Editing `mh-poster-hero-*`, not the dead `.hero-slideshow-*`
- [ ] Slide panel has `height: auto`, `align-self: start`, `aspect-ratio: var(--mh-hero-slide-ratio, 1.7778)`
- [ ] Panels grid has `min-height: 0`
- [ ] Foreground image is `object-fit: contain`; backdrop is `object-fit: cover`
- [ ] Backdrop selector includes `.mh-poster-hero-media` **and** `img` (out-specifies `filter: none`)
- [ ] Backdrop `transform: scale()` overhang exceeds the blur radius
- [ ] `onLoad` ratio capture still present on the foreground `<Image>`
- [ ] Verified in a browser at ~1280px, ~1920px and mobile — not via `curl`, the hero is client-rendered

